<p>A Bolivian military cargo plane carrying newly printed banknotes crashed while landing near El Alto International Airport outside La Paz, killing 22 people and injuring dozens, according to officials and reports. Officials and local footage described in reports said the aircraft veered off the runway and struck an avenue, damaging vehicles as it came down. The impact scattered cash across the area, and crowds tried to grab the banknotes as police moved in to secure the crash site, according to reports. Hospitals treated the injured, and officials called for blood donations—an early sign of community support amid a difficult situation.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/Ve_xH_PzXwGnK5MIr0dbWpGDZhIVZp1y0IjFz04j_UIO3PJkzrluP4y6ImpYW4wpbhu6JXlshHYTh9dRXrrzcorxrmAj-qN13SpQegdZMXhy' alt='A drone view shows the crash site on a busy avenue in El Alto, Bolivia, Feb. 28, 2026.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Cash cargo: Officials and reports said the plane carried a very large shipment of new banknotes—described as 18 tons in one account—and about $62 million in another.</li><li>Injury counts: Injury totals differed by outlet, with at least 28 injured reported by Bolivia’s health ministry in one story and 37 injured reported in another.</li><li>Crowd control details: Local footage and officials described by one report showed bystanders rushing to grab scattered cash, leading police to use tear gas; authorities were later seen burning the money at the crash site.</li><li>Detentions, voided bills: One report said authorities voided the scattered banknotes and detained dozens of people amid the chaos after the crash.</li></ul><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>Bolivia Ministry of Defence: The ministry said it will investigate what caused the crash. (<a href='https://www.dawn.com/news/1976785/22-killed-in-cash-packed-military-plane-crash-in-bolivia'>Dawn</a>)</li><li>Bolivian police (El Alto homicide division): Police officials briefed reporters late Friday with evolving casualty estimates as recovery efforts continued. (<a href='https://www.dawn.com/news/1976785/22-killed-in-cash-packed-military-plane-crash-in-bolivia'>Dawn</a>)</li><li>Bolivian Air Navigation and Airports authority (NAABOL): NAABOL reported that the flight took off from Santa Cruz and crashed during landing at El Alto. (<a href='https://www.dawn.com/news/1976785/22-killed-in-cash-packed-military-plane-crash-in-bolivia'>Dawn</a>)</li><li>Authorities handling banknotes: Officials said the scattered bills were voided after the crash and that they detained people suspected of taking them. (<a href='https://www.firstpost.com/world/bolivia-plane-crash-el-alto-international-airport-crash-bolivia-cargo-plane-crash-62-million-banknotes-scattered-13984923.html'>Firstpost</a>)</li></ul><h3>Sources:</h3><ul><li><a href='https://www.dawn.com/news/1976785/22-killed-in-cash-packed-military-plane-crash-in-bolivia'>22 killed in cash-packed military plane crash in Bolivia</a> - dawn.com</li><li><a href='https://www.firstpost.com/world/bolivia-plane-crash-el-alto-international-airport-crash-bolivia-cargo-plane-crash-62-million-banknotes-scattered-13984923.html'>Cargo plane carrying cash crashes in Bolivia, 22 dead, $62 million scattered</a> - firstpost.com</li><li><a href='https://abcnews.com/International/wireStory/death-toll-rises-22-after-cargo-plane-carrying-130634757'>Death toll rises to 22 after cargo plane carrying money crashes near Bolivia capital</a> - abcnews.com</li></ul>